.TH CTI-JIRA 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-jira \- file and follow one Jira ticket per confirmed KEV, Sev5 or CVSS 9.0+ exposure
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti-jira \-\-check
.PP
.B cti-jira \-\-test\-ticket
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.PP
.B cti-jira \-\-from\-enriched
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-follow\-up ]
.RB [ \-\-approve ]
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.PP
.B cti-jira \-\-follow\-up
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.PP
.B cti-jira \-\-domains\-actions
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.RB [ \-\-ref\-out
.IR FILE ]
.PP
.B cti-jira \-\-appscan\-tickets
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.RB [ \-\-ref\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-appscan\-reports
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-appscan\-test
.IR ID ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-jira
.I mode
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti-jira
turns confirmed exposure into tracked work: one ticket per CVE, carrying
every affected QID and host, kept current until the scanner stops finding
it. It is run by
.BR run\-digest (1)
for the daily digest only, after enrichment and before the brief, as
.PP
.EX
cti\-jira \-\-from\-enriched $FLEET_HOME/state/enriched\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.json \e
    \-\-for\-real \-\-follow\-up
.EE
.PP
and, under
.BR "run\-digest \-\-dry\-run" ,
the same without
.BR \-\-for\-real .
The operator runs
.B \-\-check
and
.B \-\-test\-ticket
by hand when setting the lane up.
.SS Safety
.TP
.B Nothing is written without \-\-for\-real
No issue is created, and no comment, attachment or issue property is
written; the ticket map is left untouched and no remediation note goes to
the findings database. A run without it makes only read calls to Jira
(search, and reading the stored exposure property) and says
.B DRY RUN
in its output.
.TP
.B Every write needs the allowlist too
Nothing is written to Jira - no new issue, no comment, no CSV attachment, no
exposure property - unless
.B JIRA_PROJECT_KEY
appears in
.B JIRA_ALLOW_CREATE
(comma-separated, case-insensitive). With the gate closed,
.B \-\-for\-real
runs read-only: existing tickets are still found and linked from the digest,
and the follow-up pass prints what it would have said.
.TP
.B Some findings wait for a person
A Sev5 that is not on CISA KEV and has a CVSS base score below 9.0 is filed
only with
.BR \-\-approve .
That is a command-line flag, never a setting, and the scheduled run does
not pass it, so the automatic path files KEV entries and CVSS 9.0+ entries
only.
.TP
.B Never transitions, except retired applications
It never closes, reopens or transitions a ticket, with one exception: the
open ticket of a scanner web app that has been deleted, under
.B \-\-appscan\-tickets
and its own gate
.BR JIRA_ALLOW_CLOSE .
Remediation, and deciding it is done, belong to the people working the ticket.
.TP
.B One ticket per CVE
Every fleet ticket carries the label
.B cti\-cve\-YYYY\-NNNN
and the search for it is scoped to the project but not to status, so a
closed ticket still counts as filed and is never duplicated.
.TP
.B The digest cannot be broken
After the configuration loads,
.B \-\-from\-enriched
and
.B \-\-follow\-up
turn every failure into a WARNING and exit 0.
.SS What it files
A finding from the enriched file earns a ticket when it is on CISA KEV,
rated Sev5 (the old P1 also counts as Sev5), or has a CVSS base score of 9.0
or more (CVSS's own Critical band), and its status is PRESENT.
KEV and CVSS 9.0+ findings are filed automatically; they are left unassigned
for triage.
Its full host list is then fetched from Qualys; a lookup that fails drops
that finding with a warning. Findings are handled worst first.
.PP
A new ticket gets a summary (prefixed
.B [OVERDUE]
once a KEV due date has passed) and description, the labels
.BR cti\-cve\-... ,
.BR cti\-agent ,
.B cisa\-kev
(when on KEV),
.B cvss\-critical
(when the CVSS base score is 9.0 or more) and
.BR sev N,
a CSV of every host
.RI ( cve\-yyyy\-nnnn\-hosts\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.csv ,
never truncated), and an issue property
.B cti\-agent\-exposure
recording what was reported.
.SS What it says on an existing ticket
The same rules apply whether the ticket was reached from today's findings
or by the follow-up, and the dry run prints the same decision the real run
acts on:
.TP
.B Closed, still detected
One comment saying so, the first time only. The ticket is not reopened.
.TP
.B No stored state
The current exposure is recorded and nothing is said: an older ticket, not
a discovery.
.TP
.B No detections remain
A comment that the ticket can be closed.
.TP
.B A new QID
A comment, at once.
.TP
.B New hosts
A comment naming up to 20 of them, with the full CSV attached, at most once
every seven days per ticket; growth in between is held for the next weekly
comment. Detections returning after an all-clear are reported at once.
.TP
.B Anything else
Including a shrinking count: recorded, no comment.
.SS The follow-up
.B \-\-follow\-up
starts from Jira rather than from the mail: every ticket in
.B JIRA_PROJECT_KEY
labelled
.B cti\-agent
that is open or was updated in the last 30 days, oldest first, at most 200
(a warning says when there were more). Tickets already handled from today's
findings, tickets without a
.B cti\-cve\-
label, and the setup test ticket are skipped. Each is looked up in Qualys,
and only a PRESENT or NOT_PRESENT answer is compared with it: an UNKNOWN or
a failed lookup leaves the ticket alone, so an outage can never post a
false all-clear.
.PP
Every fleet ticket found is added to the ticket map, so the digest links a
CVE to its ticket on any day.
.SH OPTIONS
At least one of
.BR \-\-check ,
.BR \-\-test\-ticket ,
.BR \-\-from\-enriched ,
.B \-\-follow\-up
or
.B \-\-domains\-actions
is required; with none, the usage is printed and the exit status is 1.
.B \-\-check
also prints each Jira permission the fleet uses (BROWSE_PROJECTS, CREATE_ISSUES,
ADD_COMMENTS, CREATE_ATTACHMENTS, EDIT_ISSUES, ASSIGN_ISSUES, MANAGE_WATCHERS,
LINK_ISSUES, TRANSITION_ISSUES (only when JIRA_ALLOW_CLOSE is set), and the global USER_PICKER) as ok or MISSING, without writing anything. A
MISSING permission, or permissions that cannot be read, fails the check
(exit 1). It runs first when combined with another mode, and a failed check
stops the run; no runner passes it.
.B \-\-test\-ticket
takes precedence over
.B \-\-from\-enriched
and
.BR \-\-follow\-up ,
which are then ignored.
.PP
Go's flag syntax: each option may also be written with a single dash
.RB ( \-check ),
and a value may follow
.B =
or a space.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Read-only preflight: authenticate and print the account the API token
belongs to; say whether the write gate is open for
.B JIRA_PROJECT_KEY
(a closed gate is reported, not fatal); resolve
.B JIRA_ISSUE_TYPE
in the project; list the create screen's required fields, marking with
.B <\-
any this command does not set (each would fail a create); and run the
duplicate-detection search once.
Default:\~off.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-ticket
Build an obviously synthetic ticket for
.B CVE\-1900\-00000
with hosts under
.BR .invalid .
Without
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
print it as one JSON object on standard output (mode, project, issue type,
summary, labels, search JQL, attachment name, description, CSV) and call
nothing. With
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
check the write gate, then file it (or update it, if it already exists),
attach the CSV and record its state. Its labels replace
.B cti\-agent
with
.BR cti\-agent\-test ,
so the follow-up never treats it as a real ticket. Afterwards, check by hand
that it appears in the queue your team works from.
Default:\~off.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Actually write to Jira, the ticket map and the findings database. Without
it every mode is a dry run. Has no effect on
.BR \-\-check .
Default:\~off.
.TP
.BI \-\-from\-enriched " FILE"
File and update tickets for the findings in this enriched JSON, the file
the digest's enrich step writes. A missing or unparseable file is a
warning: nothing is filed from it, and
.B \-\-follow\-up
still runs. With
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
the ticket map is written beside it (see FILES) and every ticketed CVE gets
a remediation note through
.BR "fleet\-db note" .
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.B \-\-approve
Also file new tickets for Sev5 findings that are not on CISA KEV and are
below CVSS 9.0. Without it they are logged as held for approval. Only affects new tickets.
Default:\~off.
.TP
.BI \-\-domains\-actions " FILE"
File the DNS changes in this
.B cti\-domains \-\-actions\-out
file as ONE lock-down ticket for IT (labels
.B cti\-agent
and
.BR cti\-domains\-lockdown ),
because every change needs the same registrar admin access. When a
lock-down ticket is already open, add the new list as a comment only if it
differs from the last one posted (its hash is kept on the ticket); file a
new ticket only when none is open; and when nothing is left to change, say
so once on the open ticket. The ticket separates exact records from items
to review and attaches the list as CSV. Without
.B \-\-for\-real
it prints what it would do and the ticket text. Subject to the
.B JIRA_ALLOW_CREATE
write gate. Run by
.BR run\-domains (1)
before the weekly email, so the email can name the ticket.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-appscan\-tickets " FILE"
File or update ONE ticket per application from this
.B cti\-appscan \-\-tickets\-out
file (labels
.BR cti\-agent ,
.B cti\-appscan
and
.BI cti\-appscan\- id\fR).
No open ticket and findings: file one, assigned to the owner's Jira account
(looked up by email; exactly one active match or it stays unassigned, and
the report says so), with the owner's component if one is given. If Jira
refuses the assignee or component, it is filed without them. Open ticket
and the findings changed: one comment listing what is new (in full) and what
is no longer detected. When the new ticket is for an application that had
an earlier closed ticket, the description says "Recurring" with that ticket's
key and how many of the findings were on it, and the new ticket is linked to it
with a Jira "Relates" link (needs "Link issues"; a refused link is logged and
the ticket stands). The preview prints the note and a "would link to" line.
Same findings: nothing. Nothing left: say so once.
A web app marked
.B covers=
in app-owners.txt (a staging copy scanned instead of production) gets a
"Stands in for" line saying the fix goes into the code and is then
deployed. An application marked to be left alone is not touched. Every finding says
where it is, the risk, what the scanner found and how to fix it, and the
full list is attached as text. Without
.B \-\-for\-real
it prints what it would file. Subject to the
.B JIRA_ALLOW_CREATE
write gate; up to ten minutes. The API account needs "Browse users and
groups" to look owners up. A
.B watchers=
field in app-owners.txt (up to 10 emails) adds each as a watcher on the
ticket, on create and on every weekly comment; one not found, invalid or
refused is logged and skipped. That needs "Manage watcher list". Run by
.BR run\-appscan (1)
before the weekly email.
.IP
Besides the confirmed findings, a ticket lists the scanner's
.B POTENTIAL
(unconfirmed) findings of severity 4 (Critical) and 5 (Urgent) in their own
section, "POTENTIAL - please verify", under a callout saying they are
deliberately listed, are not confirmed, and that not confirmed does not mean
not there. Each line is labelled "POTENTIAL \fIsev\fR - unconfirmed, please
verify", and the header and summary count them separately ("To verify").
Potential findings of Serious and below are left out. They are part of the
findings fingerprint, so an open ticket gets one comment when they first
appear.
.IP
Retired applications: when a web app has been deleted from the scanner, its
open ticket is closed with an explanatory comment. Guards: it happens only
when
.BR cti\-appscan
fetched the scanner's complete web-app list in that run (the
.B scannerApps
field of the tickets file; a failed or skipped fetch closes nothing); only
open tickets labelled
.B cti\-appscan
and
.BI cti\-appscan\- id
with a numeric scanner id are considered (name-keyed applications are never
closed) and that carry the fleet's own state property; the list must agree with
that run's scans and the tickets file must be under 24 hours old; at most 3
tickets are closed per run and never most of the open queue, and more would-be
closures close none and log a warning (it looks like lost scanner visibility,
not retirements); a full search page (100 open tickets) also skips. Closing
needs its own gate,
.BR JIRA_ALLOW_CLOSE ,
independent of
.BR JIRA_ALLOW_CREATE ,
and
.BR \-\-for\-real ;
without
.B \-\-for\-real
the preview prints "would close KEY via transition ...". The transition is
chosen by its destination status category (Done), not by name; if several
lead there,
.B JIRA_CLOSE_TRANSITION
names the one to use. A transition that requires a field other than
resolution or comment is refused; a resolution field on the screen is set to
"Done"; the comment goes in the same request. Needs the Jira permission
"Transition issues". Closing is reversible by reopening; if the application
returns with findings, a new ticket is filed and linked to the closed one.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-appscan\-reports " DIR"
With
.BR \-\-appscan\-tickets :
a
.BR cti\-appreport (1)
output directory. Each application's zipped scanner report listed in
.IR DIR /reports.json
is attached when its ticket is filed and when its findings change; the
ticket text then says it is attached and that it contains scan-account
session cookies. An application with no listed report is filed or commented
as usual. The reports are not deleted by this command.
Without
.B \-\-for\-real
the preview ends each would-create and would-comment with "would attach
appscan-DATE.txt (N KB)" and, when
.I DIR
holds that application's report, "would attach qualys-was-report-DATE.zip
(N KB)", otherwise "no scanner report to attach".
Default:\~empty (nothing attached).
.TP
.BI \-\-appscan\-test " ID"
With
.B \-\-appscan\-tickets
(and usually
.BR \-\-appscan\-reports ):
rehearse the ticket for one scanner web-app
.IR ID .
Without
.B \-\-for\-real
it previews; with it, files ONE new ticket whose summary starts "[TEST]",
labelled
.B cti\-agent\-test
only (so the weekly run never finds or comments on it and the Jira follow-up
skips it), unassigned, with no component, no watchers and nothing recorded in
state, carrying the findings text and the scanner report as attachments.
The
.B JIRA_ALLOW_CREATE
gate applies. Refuses an
.I ID
that is not in the file, or one the weekly run would leave alone. The ticket
is yours to delete in Jira afterwards; the report holds scan-account session
cookies.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-appscan\-close\-test " KEY"
Rehearse closing a retired application's ticket. Without
.B \-\-for\-real
it previews the transition and comment; with it, closes ONE ticket, and only
one filed by
.B \-\-appscan\-test
(label
.BR cti\-agent\-test ).
It refuses any other ticket, including the weekly run's own. The
.B JIRA_ALLOW_CLOSE
gate applies.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-ref\-out " FILE"
With
.BR \-\-domains\-actions :
write what happened to the lock-down ticket as JSON, mode 0600 - the
action
.RB ( created ,
.BR commented ,
.BR unchanged ,
.BR resolved ,
.BR none ,
.BR would\-create ,
.BR would\-comment ,
.BR would\-resolve ,
.BR gate\-closed ,
.BR error ),
the key, its browse URL, its status and, on failure, why. Written on every
path, failures included, for
.BR "cti\-domains \-\-stamp\-ticket" .
Default:\~not written.
.TP
.B \-\-follow\-up
Re-check every open fleet ticket against the scanner as described above,
after today's findings. On its own (no
.BR \-\-from\-enriched )
no ticket map is written. Raises the run's time limit from two minutes to
fifteen.
Default:\~off.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is loaded at start from
.B FLEET_ENV
(else
.IR $FLEET_HOME/fleet.env ,
else
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent/fleet.env );
variables already in the environment win.
.TP
.BR JIRA_BASE_URL ", " JIRA_EMAIL ", " JIRA_API_TOKEN
The Jira site and the account the token belongs to; the token carries all
of that account's permissions. Required.
.I fleet.env
section 9.
.TP
.B JIRA_CLOUD_ID
Optional. The site's cloud id (a UUID only; see
.IR https://SITE.atlassian.net/_edge/tenant_info ).
When set, API calls go through
.I https://api.atlassian.com/ex/jira/CLOUD_ID
as an Atlassian scoped API token requires; links in email still use
.BR JIRA_BASE_URL .
Unset, both use
.BR JIRA_BASE_URL .
.TP
.B JIRA_PROJECT_KEY
The project tickets are searched for and filed in. Required, no default.
Section 9.
.TP
.B JIRA_ISSUE_TYPE
The issue type name exactly as the project spells it. Required, no default.
Section 9.
.TP
.B JIRA_ALLOW_CREATE
The write allowlist. Optional; empty means write nothing to Jira.
Section 9.
.TP
.B JIRA_ALLOW_CLOSE
The close allowlist: comma-separated project keys in which a retired
application's ticket may be closed (see
.BR \-\-appscan\-tickets ).
Optional; empty means never close. Independent of
.BR JIRA_ALLOW_CREATE .
Section 9.
.TP
.B JIRA_CLOSE_TRANSITION
Optional. The name (case-insensitive) of the transition to use when several
lead to a Done-category status. Section 9.
.TP
.BR QUALYS_BASE_URL ", " QUALYS_USERNAME ", " QUALYS_PASSWORD
The scanner the host lists come from. Required when
.B LOOKUP_PROVIDER
is
.B qualys
(the default); the lookups always use Qualys. Section 2.
.TP
.B QUALYS_KB_CACHE
The CVE-to-QID cache. With
.B \-\-from\-enriched
or
.B \-\-follow\-up
it is loaded (or built) before any finding or ticket is looked at; if that
fails, nothing is filed and no ticket is
re-checked; the run warns and exits 0. A relative path resolves against the
working directory.
Default:\~\fI./qualys_kb_cache.json\fR. Section 3.
.TP
.B QUALYS_KB_MAX_AGE_HOURS
How long the cache is trusted. Default:\~\fB168\fR. Section 3.
.TP
.B FLEET_CODE
Where
.B bin/fleet\-db
is found for the remediation note. Unset: no note, silently (off the fleet
host). Not absolute, or no regular file named
.B fleet\-db
there: a warning.
.TP
.BR GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS ", " MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS ", " MAILBOX_MIN_AGE_HOURS
Not used, but validated by the shared configuration loader; an invalid
value stops the run. See
.BR cti\-agent (1).
.TP
.BR FLEET_ENV ", " FLEET_HOME
Locate
.IR fleet.env .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env
Jira and Qualys credentials.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/enriched\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.json
The day's enriched findings, written by the digest's enrich step.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/enriched\-YYYY\-MM\-DD\-tickets.json
The CVE-to-ticket map
.BR brief.py
reads to link findings to tickets. Written atomically, 0600, only with
.BR \-\-for\-real ;
named after the
.B \-\-from\-enriched
file, so replaying an old day reads that day's tickets.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/qualys_kb_cache.json
The KnowledgeBase cache, 0600.
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/bin/fleet\-db
Called as
.B fleet\-db note
.I CVE
.I "KEY URL (filed YYYY-MM-DD)"
so the findings table stops reporting a ticketed CVE as un-actioned.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. With
.B \-\-from\-enriched
or
.BR \-\-follow\-up ,
always once the configuration has loaded: a missing enriched file, an
unavailable scanner cache, failed searches, creates, comments or
attachments, and an unwritten ticket map are all warnings.
.TP
.B 1
No mode was given; configuration missing or invalid (the message names the
variables);
.B \-\-check
failed (credentials rejected, issue type not found, fields or search
unavailable); or
.B \-\-test\-ticket \-\-for\-real
failed (write gate closed, search, create or comment failed).
.TP
.B 2
An unknown option.
.B \-\-help
prints the usage and exits 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set-up, in order: read-only checks, the ticket as it would be filed, then
one real test ticket:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-jira \-\-check
sudo cti\-agent cti\-jira \-\-test\-ticket
sudo cti\-agent cti\-jira \-\-test\-ticket \-\-for\-real
.EE
.PP
What tomorrow's run would say to every open ticket, writing nothing:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-jira \-\-follow\-up
.EE
.PP
File today's held Sev5 findings (not KEV, CVSS below 9.0) after reviewing them:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-jira \-\-approve \e
    \-\-from\-enriched /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/enriched\-$(date +%F).json
sudo cti\-agent cti\-jira \-\-approve \-\-for\-real \e
    \-\-from\-enriched /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/enriched\-$(date +%F).json
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-digest (1),
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
